w("### 1c. Target scaling, outlier handling, and the SI's own architecture (50 seeds each, as-coded restore)")
w()
w("Variants fixed in advance: `target_scaled` (temperatures standardised for training, predictions mapped back), `clip5` (standardised features clipped to +-5), `drop_extreme_rows` (training rows with any |z| > 10 removed; refit scaler), `quantile` (QuantileTransformer to normal instead of StandardScaler), `target_scaled+clip5`. **Post hoc**, added after seeing that quantile and target scaling each closed part of the gap: `quantile+target_scaled`, and the SI Table S5 architecture (ReLU, dropout 0.124, hidden 128-512-2048-256-1024, lr 0.002, 100 epochs) as `S5`, `S5+target_scaled`, `S5+quantile+target_scaled`. These are **diagnostics of what could explain the SI's number on the SI's own test set, not model selection**; no variant is adopted as the project's model.")
w()
w("| variant | R2 mean (sd) [min-max] | 90th / 99th pct | RMSE mean | MAE mean | runs with R2 >= 0.41 | share of the gap closed |")
w("|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|")
order = ["baseline", "target_scaled", "clip5", "drop_extreme_rows", "quantile", "target_scaled+clip5", "quantile+target_scaled", "S5", "S5+target_scaled", "S5+quantile+target_scaled"]
rows = {}
for v in order:
    r = load(v)
    if r is None:
        continue
    rows[v] = r
    gc = (r.r2.mean() - base.r2.mean()) / (0.41 - base.r2.mean())
    tag = " (post hoc)" if v in ("quantile+target_scaled", "S5", "S5+target_scaled", "S5+quantile+target_scaled") else ""
    w(f"| {v}{tag} | {r.r2.mean():.3f} ({r.r2.std():.3f}) [{r.r2.min():.3f}-{r.r2.max():.3f}] | {r.r2.quantile(.9):.3f} / {r.r2.quantile(.99):.3f} | {r.rmse.mean():.1f} | {r.mae.mean():.1f} | {int((r.r2 >= .41).sum())}/{len(r)} | {100 * gc:.0f}% |")
w("| SI | 0.41 | | 67 | 47 | | 100% |")
w()
s5t = rows.get("S5+target_scaled")
if s5t is not None:
    w("**Which, if any, explains the gap.**")
    w(f"- Not seed variation (section 1b) and not the saved checkpoints (section 1a).")
    w(f"- No single change to the **released** recipe reaches 0.41: target scaling {rows['target_scaled'].r2.mean():.3f}, quantile features {rows['quantile'].r2.mean():.3f} (best single run {rows['quantile'].r2.max():.3f}), feature clipping {rows['clip5'].r2.mean():.3f}; dropping extreme-feature training rows makes it worse. Heavy-tailed features clearly hurt the released network on this data (clipping or a quantile transform helps), but the SI states z-scoring (StandardScaler) was used, so these are sensitivity results, not a reconstruction of the authors' pipeline.")
    w(f"- **The SI's own Table S5 architecture (ReLU, dropout 0.124, hidden 128-512-2048-256-1024, lr 0.002, 100 epochs) trained on standardised temperatures reproduces the SI's thermal numbers**: R2 {s5t.r2.mean():.3f} ({s5t.r2.std():.3f}), RMSE {s5t.rmse.mean():.1f}, MAE {s5t.mae.mean():.1f} against the SI's 0.41 / 67 / 47, and **{int((s5t.r2 >= .41).sum())} of {len(s5t)} runs ({100 * (s5t.r2 >= .41).mean():.0f}%) have R2 >= 0.41** (best {s5t.r2.max():.3f}). Neither ingredient alone does it: the S5 network on raw temperatures gives {rows['S5'].r2.mean():.3f}, the released network on standardised temperatures {rows['target_scaled'].r2.mean():.3f}.")
    w("- **How far to trust this.** It is a post hoc diagnostic on the SI's own test set, found by trying ten variants; I report all of them and adopt none. What supports it beyond the R2 match is that three SI metrics (R2, RMSE, MAE) are matched at once and that the variation across seeds (sd 0.024) makes 0.41 an ordinary draw. What it cannot show: the SI never mentions target standardisation, the released training script trains on raw temperatures, and other unknown differences could produce the same numbers. The reading that fits all evidence is that the SI's thermal model is the Table S5 network trained on standardised temperatures, **not** the checkpoint or the training script in the release (which give R2 0.14-0.28).")
    w()
# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 2
J = pd.read_parquet(D + "joint_cascade_pvalues.parquet")
npos_rep = J.groupby("rep").all_stable.sum().iloc[0]
w("## 2. Joint cascade guarantee (jackknife+ style cross-conformal on the all-three-stable MOFs)")
w()
w("**Joint score.** For a MOF x with stage scores s_1 (thermal: predicted temperature), s_2 (solvent: P(stable)), s_3 (water: P(class 2) + P(class 3)), each stage score is rank-normalised on that stage's calibration data (mid-rank ECDF of the out-of-fold scores of all stage-labelled MOFs in the calibration pool) and the **joint score is the minimum** of the three rank-normalised scores, c(x) = min_k u_k(s_k(x)). A MOF is kept when c(x) is not too low relative to the all-stable MOFs.")
w()
w("**Calibration (CV+ / jackknife+ style).** The calibration positives are the MOFs that are stable at all three stages (17 of the 92 labelled in all three tables, in 14 distinct groups). Each calibration positive i has an out-of-fold joint score c_i from the models trained without its group; for a new MOF x every such fold-model j gives a score c^(j)(x). With n calibration positives, p(x) = (1 + #{i : c_i <= c^(j(i))(x)}) / (n + 1) and **x is kept iff p(x) > alpha** (ties count towards keeping). `src/conformal/joint.py`, tests `tests/test_joint.py`.")
w()
w("**Exact guarantee (what is and is not claimed).**")
w("- Theorem being borrowed: Barber, Candes, Ramdas, Tibshirani (2021), jackknife+ / CV+. For exchangeable calibration and test points and a symmetric fitting procedure, a test point is excluded from the prediction set with probability at most 2 alpha, i.e. **recall >= 1 - 2 alpha** for the population the calibration positives are drawn from (here: MOFs stable at all three stages); K-fold CV+ with equal folds has an extra slack min{2(1-1/K)/(n/K+1), (1-K/n)/(K+1)} (zero for leave-one-out). I adapted the one-sided 'positive-like' form myself and did not re-prove it.")
w("- Conditions that are assumptions here, not facts: (i) the all-stable MOFs are exchangeable (they come from 14 groups with within-group dependence, and are the MOFs that happen to carry all three labels); (ii) the fold models are fit by a symmetric procedure; here each fold leaves out one calibration group (some folds contain two positives) and a random share of the remaining groups, so it is group-level leave-out, not exact leave-one-out; (iii) the test MOFs are exchangeable with the calibration positives (not tested under shift: `notes/phase5.md` section 3).")
w("- Compared with the union bound, the guarantee here is **for the population of interest itself** (the all-stable MOFs), so it does not need the extra assumption that per-stage coverage carries over to that sub-population (`notes/review.md` H3). The cost is that n is only 17 (14 independent groups).")
w()
w("**Largest recall target 17 positives can support.** The rule is non-trivial only if alpha >= 1/(n + 1); below that every MOF is kept.")
w()
w("| calibration units | n | smallest non-trivial alpha | largest guaranteed recall 1 - 2 alpha | largest marginal split-conformal level 1 - alpha |")
w("|---|---|---|---|---|")
for nm, n in (("all-stable MOFs (positives)", 17), ("their groups (conservative)", 14)):
    w(f"| {nm} | {n} | {1 / (n + 1):.4f} | {1 - 2 / (n + 1):.3f} | {1 - 1 / (n + 1):.3f} |")
w()
w(f"So with 17 positives the largest recall the jackknife+ guarantee can certify with a non-trivial filter is **0.889** (alpha = 1/18); counted by groups (14) it is 0.867. In the nested evaluation below the calibration pool for each test fold has n = {int(J.n_cal_pos.min())}-{int(J.n_cal_pos.max())} positives (median {int(J.n_cal_pos.median())}), so its smallest non-trivial alpha is higher (about {1 / (J.n_cal_pos.median() + 1):.3f}); the first row of the table is accordingly almost trivial.")
w()
w(f"**Evaluation (nested, nothing tuned on the test MOFs).** Stage models: random forests (100 trees, fixed in advance; not the Phase 2/3 models, so numbers are not comparable to Fig. 4). Outer: 10 random group folds of all 4,963 MOFs, test = the labelled-in-all-three MOFs of the fold (excluded from every model and from calibration); inner: one fold per calibration positive's group. 3 repetitions (outer seeds 0-2). Each repetition scores each of the 92 labelled MOFs (17 positives, 75 others) once; the three repetitions re-use the same MOFs, so pooled counts (51 positive evaluations) are **not independent**, and Clopper-Pearson intervals below are optimistic.")
w()
w("| alpha | guaranteed recall 1 - 2 alpha | joint: recall (passes) [95% CP] | joint: kept of 92 | joint: false pass of 75 | union bound: recall (passes) [95% CP] | union: kept | union: false pass |")
w("|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|")
for a in (1 / 18, 0.075, 0.10, 0.15, 0.20, 0.25, 0.30):
    kj = J.p_joint > a; ku = (J.p_thermal > a / 3) & (J.p_solvent > a / 3) & (J.p_water > a / 3); pos = J.all_stable; cells = []
    for k in (kj, ku):
        kp, n = int((k & pos).sum()), int(pos.sum()); lo, hi = cp(kp, n)
        cells.append(f"{kp / n:.3f} ({kp}/{n}) [{lo:.2f}, {hi:.2f}] | {k.mean():.3f} | {k[~pos].mean():.3f}")
    w(f"| {a:.4f} | {1 - 2 * a:.3f} | " + " | ".join(cells) + " |")
w()
w("The union-bound version here is the same cross-conformal rule applied to each stage's own stable MOFs with alpha / 3 per stage (so its stage-wise guarantee, if each stage's coverage carried over to the all-stable MOFs, would also be 1 - 2 alpha, ignoring the K-fold slack and the union over stages' own CV+ error).")
w()
j10, u10 = J.p_joint > 0.10, (J.p_thermal > 0.10 / 3) & (J.p_solvent > 0.10 / 3) & (J.p_water > 0.10 / 3)
w(f"**Reading.** (i) Realised recall is above the guaranteed 1 - 2 alpha at every alpha, as expected for a bound that is loose by construction (the 2 alpha factor); at alpha = 0.10 the joint rule has recall {(j10 & J.all_stable).sum() / J.all_stable.sum():.3f} against a guarantee of 0.80. (ii) At the same alpha the joint score keeps clearly fewer MOFs than the union bound (alpha = 0.10: {j10.mean():.3f} vs {u10.mean():.3f} of the 92) at a somewhat lower, still above-guarantee, recall ({(j10 & J.all_stable).sum() / J.all_stable.sum():.3f} vs {(u10 & J.all_stable).sum() / J.all_stable.sum():.3f}); the union bound spends recall it is not asked for. (iii) Neither rule filters much at a high target: the smallest-alpha rows keep 93-95% of the 92 MOFs, as expected with so few positives. (iv) The kept fraction among MOFs that are not stable at all three stages ('false pass') is high for both rules (0.78 vs 0.88 at alpha = 0.10): with this much calibration noise and weak stage models (group-aware AUC 0.6-0.8) the cascade removes only a modest share of the unstable MOFs. (v) These are 17 MOFs; the data are consistent with the guarantee, but with 17 distinct MOFs (14 groups) and three repetitions of the same MOFs they have little power to contradict it.")
w()
# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 2b
JG = pd.read_parquet(D + "joint_cascade_group_pvalues.parquet")
JF = pd.read_parquet(D + "joint_cascade_pvalues_first_run.parquet")
same = bool(np.allclose(JG.sort_values(["rep", "name"]).p_joint.values, JF.sort_values(["rep", "name"]).p_joint.values))
w("### 2b. Calibrating at the group level (the 14 groups as units, not the 17 MOFs)")
w()
w(f"The 17 all-stable MOFs lie in 14 groups, so 17 overstates the number of independent calibration units. Here each group of all-stable MOFs is **one calibration unit**: its calibration score is the **minimum** of the joint scores of its positives (primary: the lowest score can only keep more MOFs, so this is the conservative choice for recall) or their **mean** (sensitivity); each group is its own fold, so n = number of groups (14 for all 17 positives; {int(JG.n_cal_groups.min())}-{int(JG.n_cal_groups.max())} in the nested folds), and the rule is p(x) = (1 + #{{groups g : c_g <= c^(g)(x)}}) / (n + 1), keep iff p > alpha. Same nested evaluation as section 2 (the run was repeated to store the group-level p-values; its MOF-level p-values reproduce the first run exactly: {same}). Both variants were fixed before looking at results.")
w()
w("**Supportable targets.** With 14 units the smallest non-trivial alpha is 1/15 = 0.0667, so the jackknife+ guarantee 1 - 2 alpha can certify at most **0.867** (the MOF-level count of 17 gives 0.889). In the nested folds the pool has as few as " + f"{int(JG.n_cal_groups.min())} groups (smallest non-trivial alpha {1 / (JG.n_cal_groups.max() + 1):.3f} to {1 / (JG.n_cal_groups.min() + 1):.3f}), so the first rows are partly trivial.")
w()
w("| alpha | guaranteed recall 1 - 2 alpha | MOF-level (17): recall, kept | group-level min: recall (passes) [95% CP] | kept of 92 | false pass of 75 | group-level mean: recall, kept |")
w("|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|")
pos = JG.all_stable; npos = int(pos.sum())
for a in (1 / 15, 0.075, 0.10, 0.15, 0.20, 0.25, 0.30):
    km, kn, k17 = JG.p_group_min > a, JG.p_group_mean > a, JG.p_joint > a
    kp = int((km & pos).sum()); lo, hi = cp(kp, npos)
    w(f"| {a:.4f} | {1 - 2 * a:.3f} | {(k17 & pos).sum() / npos:.3f}, {k17.mean():.3f} | {kp / npos:.3f} ({kp}/{npos}) [{lo:.2f}, {hi:.2f}] | {km.mean():.3f} | {km[~pos].mean():.3f} | {(kn & pos).sum() / npos:.3f}, {kn.mean():.3f} |")
w()
a10 = 0.10
_al = (1 / 15, 0.075, 0.10, 0.15, 0.20, 0.25, 0.30)
_kg = [(JG.p_group_min > a).mean() for a in _al]; _km = [(JG.p_joint > a).mean() for a in _al]
_more = sum(g > m + 1e-12 for g, m in zip(_kg, _km)); _eq = sum(abs(g - m) < 1e-12 for g, m in zip(_kg, _km)); _less = sum(g < m - 1e-12 for g, m in zip(_kg, _km))
w(f"**Reading.** (i) Counting groups instead of MOFs lowers the largest certifiable target from 0.889 to 0.867. The group-level (minimum) rule keeps at least as many of the 92 MOFs as the MOF-level rule at the listed alphas (more at {_more} of {len(_al)}, equal at {_eq}, fewer at {_less}; e.g. {_kg[0]:.3f} vs {(JG.p_joint > _al[0]).mean():.3f} at alpha = 1/15 and {_kg[3]:.3f} vs {_km[3]:.3f} at alpha = 0.15, identical at alpha = 0.10), and its recall is equal or higher, so it is the more conservative rule where the two differ; the differences are up to {round(92 * max(abs(g - m) for g, m in zip(_kg, _km)))} of the 92 MOFs (at the smallest alpha) and {round(92 * max(abs(g - m) for g, m in zip(_kg[1:], _km[1:])))} or fewer at the others (the evaluation has 92 MOFs and 51 positive evaluations). (ii) The group-level guarantee is the more defensible statement, because the positives within a group are not independent; the price is a coarser threshold (14 order statistics instead of 17) and a lower ceiling on the certifiable recall. (iii) The mean-of-group variant keeps fewer MOFs than the minimum variant (e.g. {(JG.p_group_mean > 0.10).mean():.3f} vs {(JG.p_group_min > 0.10).mean():.3f} at alpha = 0.10), with equal or lower recall, and is not covered by the same conservativeness argument. (iv) As in section 2, recall is above 1 - 2 alpha at every alpha, 17 MOFs / 14 groups give little power to contradict the guarantee, and the repetitions share the same MOFs.")
w()
