w("- **The ~270 MB file is not structures.** `TSA.zip` (271.7 MB) in the CoRE MOF 2024 record (Zenodo 15055758) holds temperature-swing-adsorption CSVs (371 CSV, 5 CIF, from its zip directory read with HTTP range requests; nothing was downloaded to look). The record's only structure archive (`CoREMOF2024DB_SI_20250204.zip`, 44 MB, 8,300 CIFs) uses 2024 ids (`0000[Co][nan]2[ASR]1.cif`), `mofid-v2.zip` (4.5 MB) holds MOFid outputs for the 2024 MOFs, `water.zip` holds adsorption isotherm files. **None contains an optimof refcode.** The CoRE 2024 CSVs share 0 refcodes/names with the optimof tables and only 25 of the solvent table's 1,174 DOIs (57 CoRE rows), so there is no free MOFid for the optimof MOFs.")
w("- The optimof MOFs are named by their **CoRE MOF 2019** CIFs (`ABAVIJ_clean.cif`; the thermal and solvent tables carry the name in `CoRE_name`). With your approval I downloaded the CoRE MOF 2019 ASR archive (Zenodo 3677685, `2019-11-01-ASR-public_12020.tar.gz`, 44.8 MB; md5 verified against Zenodo; 12,020 CIFs; no unsafe paths; extracted to `data/raw/coremof2019/cifs`, 266 MB). The FSR archive (27.2 MB) was not needed.")
w("- **MOFid is not on PyPI.** Built from source (`snurr-group/mofid`, commit `3687368`, GPL-2.0; `data/raw/mofid_src`, gitignored) with gcc 13.3 inside the `mof-stability` env only: installed `cmake`, `make`, `swig`, `openjdk` (conda-forge, about 156 MB of downloads; pinned in `environment.yml`). **CMake 4.x fails on Open Babel's CMakeLists (policies removed), so `cmake<4` (3.31.8) is required.** `make init` built Open Babel and the `sbu` binary; `pip install .` installed the Python wrapper (`mofid 1.1.0`). Tested on three CIFs before the batch (3-8 s each). This is MOFid-v1; topology comes from Systre via Java.")
w()
